<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gers</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gers is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, named after the river Gers, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auch">Auch</a>. A rural area of hills, it covers the historic Armagnac district, known for its brandy, and in antiquity was home to the Ausci and Elusates.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gers.jpg" alt="Gers" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Didier Descouens · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Auch)_Fa%C3%A7ade_ouest_de_la_pr%C3%A9fecture_du_Gers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
